# Changelog

We do not follow semantic versioning.
This file is mostly filled with the help of commit history by AI assistance.
Note that some changes may be not written here.

---

## [1.9.0] — 2026-09-xx

### Highlights
- Major documentation and repository cleanup, with a simpler project structure.
- Added Lean-aware file inspection and a new `xlade error` command.
- Clarified the project scope, supported environment, modes, security model, and long-term roadmap.

### Added
- Lean-aware file inspection and the `xlade error` command with human-readable hints.
- Expanded documentation for agents, AI use, project scope, installation, metrics, modes, runtime state, and security.
- Beginner-oriented articles, consolidated GitHub templates, and a v2–v3 roadmap.

### Changed
- Simplified the repository and moved/reorganized documentation around the current project scope.
- Clarified Linux support, mode defaults, security guidance, contribution instructions, and experiment documentation.
- Removed unused examples, platforms, policies, source files, demos, and obsolete documentation.

---

## [1.8.0] — 2026-08-18

### Highlights
- Expanded the experiment framework with two new experiments and a clearer metadata and metrics model.
- Strengthened kernel-protection checks and added regression coverage for security-sensitive behavior.
- Improved repository validation, cleanup, and mirror/release automation.

### Added
- EXP-006 (Teorth Analysis) and EXP-007 (And/Or), with their metadata, runners, proofs, and metrics.
- A shared experiment schema and metrics documentation.
- Three DCVE security advisories and expanded validation, kernel-protection, and workflow tests.

### Changed
- Simplified experiment documentation and standardized metrics across EXP-005 through EXP-007.
- Strengthened kernel-diff protection and mirror/SourceForge synchronization workflows.
- Updated CI, documentation, security policy, and release configuration; removed obsolete assets and status material.
- Fixed EXP-005 so it respects its committed Lean toolchain.

---

## [1.7.0] — 2026-07-11

### Highlights
- Added EXP-004 and EXP-005 experiment content and documentation.
- Strengthened formatting checks, CI, release automation, and repository hygiene.

### Added
- Automated SourceForge synchronization and repository-level formatting configuration.
- EXP-004: Project Proof 1 and EXP-005: Lean4 Courses documentation.

### Changed
- Pinned CI workflows to Python 3.14 and excluded the Lean submodule from repository formatting checks.
- Simplified packaging and ignore rules, and updated the README and logo.

### Notes
- No new user-facing CLI features were added in this release.

---

## [1.6.0] — 2026-06-01

### Highlights
- Overhauled CLI output and added a shared Lean execution layer.
- Added integration CI/CD and expanded the test suite across supported environments.
- Reworked the project documentation, installation guidance, and security documentation.

### Added
- `xlade/core/lean.py` for shared Lean and Lake execution, version detection, and result handling.
- Integration CI, release automation, and a dedicated kernel-protection workflow.
- Tests for the Lean execution layer and updated CLI behavior.
- Release, installation, CLI, end-to-end, and security documentation.

### Changed
- Standardized CLI formatting and added clearer diagnostics, summaries, and setup guidance.
- Routed experiment execution through the shared Lean layer, with graceful handling when Lake or Elan is unavailable.
- Updated workflows with read permissions and improved mirror failure reporting.
- Verified installation and experiment execution on Linux, FreeBSD, and Android/Termux.

### Notes
- CLI output changed significantly; tools parsing xLaDe output may need updates.
- EXP-001 skips cleanly when Lake is unavailable.

---

## [1.5.0] — 2026-05-26

### Highlights
- xLaDe became an installable Python package with a functional CLI.
- EXP-002 and EXP-003 began executing for real, with run history recorded in metrics.
- Added a 50-test suite and new metrics and status commands.

### Added
- Python packaging via `pyproject.toml` and an isolated test suite.
- Persistent run metrics with `xlade metrics` and `xlade status` summaries.
- EXP-003: Documentation Coverage Check, plus real execution paths for script- and Lean-based experiments.
- Contributor AI-use guidance and the initial versioned roadmap.

### Changed
- Experiment runs now record their ID, mode, toolchain, timestamp, and status.
- Lean experiments fall back to a skipped result when Lake is unavailable.
- Updated enabled-experiment configuration, contributor files, and repository ignore rules.

### Notes
- The CLI remains experimental, but is functional and tested.

---

## [1.4.0] — 2026-04-27

### Highlights
- Added metadata for each experiment to support backward compatibility in the future (ongoing long-term idea)
- Modified CLI tool to run experiments

### Added
- Mirrors to support decentralization and reduce redundancy
- Structured tools module
- Docs explaining roadmap regarding backward compatibility

### Notes
This monthly release makes the CLI tool execute experiments, but the testing of the xLaDe CLI hasn't been done properly, so problems may occur.

---

## [1.3.0] — 2026-03-31

### Highlights
- Made a functioning official site of xLaDe available at "http://xladeajfgkh32qgq5sj2mtmho3te5pivto7lav44dsbov6uduciz6hqd.onion/"
- Added project on GitLab at "https://gitlab.com/lakshitsinghbishttm/xLaDe". Both GitHub and GitLab versions will be synced.

### Added
- Rationale behind choosing an onion site for xLaDe.
- New examples of Lean to try in xLaDe.
- Dedicated security folder.

### Changed
- Docs to focus on the xLaDe functioning, limitations, contributions and README.

### Notes
This monthly release focuses mainly on organization and official site of xLaDe and its integration with GitHub repo instead of major code changes. xLaDe still is in its experimental stage.

---

## [1.2.0] — 2026-02-20

### Highlights
- License changed to GNU General Public License v3.0 (GPL-3.0).
- Documentation changes

### Added
- Expanded architectural documentation and rationale.
- Clearer separation between trusted infrastructure and experimental layers.
- Additional governance and status documentation.
- Improved contributor-facing guidance.

### Changed
- README rewritten to reflect scope and limitations.
- Documentation reorganized for clarity and traceability.
- Experiment layouts and metadata made more consistent.
- Policies and modes aligned with documented architecture.

### Notes
This release consolidates xLaDe’s structure and documentation.
It does not introduce new experiments or tooling features.
All components remain experimental.

---

## [1.1.0] — 2026-01-24

### Highlights
- Transition from conceptual framework to enforceable ecosystem platform.
- Formalized experiments, modes, policies, and metrics as stable components.

### Added
- Experiment lifecycle and template.
- EXP-001: Enforced Proof Review (Lean-based policy).
- EXP-002: Kernel Boundary Violation Detection (CI-enforced).
- Build modes: onboarding, experimental, stable.
- Metrics framework for ecosystem-level evaluation.
- Repository-wide kernel protection via CI.
- Public-facing documentation for contributors and users.

### Changed
- README updated to reflect xLaDe as an executable ecosystem platform.
- Contribution workflow reoriented around experiments.

### Notes
This release stabilizes the xLaDe ecosystem framework.
All features remain experimental, but their structure and enforcement
mechanisms are considered stable.

---

## [1.0.0] — 2025-12-31

### Highlights
- Initial public release of xLaDe.
- Established xLaDe as an experimental Lean 4 ecosystem framework.

### Added
- Core repository structure (`docs/`, `examples/`, etc.).
- Formal project documentation and README.
- Forked and integrated the official Lean 4 Theorem Prover as a submodule.
- Contribution guidelines and initial contributor list.
- GitHub project essentials:
  - issue templates
  - pull request templates
  - badges
  - `.gitignore`

### Notes
This release represents the foundational structure of xLaDe.
It is experimental and not intended for production use.

---
